{"auditVerdictAtHead":"pass","canonicalDigest":"sha256:55b111e24fa5ac40d2fbc098a937f3b4c61d2c4d4d727e520096ee1488af1f33","canonicalizationVersion":"1","changeRequest":{"author":"VykosMolt","body":"omarchy-system-lock now waits for a shell-issued receipt showing that its lock request became secure. The shell records that outcome before authentication can unlock again and retains it after unlock, so a fast authentication cannot cause the script to re-lock. Concurrent callers share the active request; tokens belong to one shell incarnation, and an unrelated or stale receipt cannot prove success. Existing lock lock IPC replies remain compatible.\n\nRefused, unavailable, lost and accepted-but-never-secure requests fail with a critical notification request. The screensaver closes only after a matching secured receipt and stays up on failure. Active pending/secured receipts stay available while their lock is held. On release, each immutable outcome is archived for thirty wall-clock seconds and expires on result reads as well as new requests. The archive window starts at release, so a caller joining a long-held lock can still confirm a fast unlock. A forward clock jump can expire an archive conservatively, without turning it into another request's success.\n\nValidation: all 48 system-lock contract checks and diff checks pass at 3a590ca0. Independent reviews also exercised mutation, restart/prototype rejection and 2,000-cycle bounded churn probes. At the preceding 55651115, all nine related lock/sleep-lock test files and qmllint passed. An activated Omarchy 4.0.4 disposable overlay verified the installed candidate hashes and shell source path. Native checks cover a healthy lock (0 in 756 ms), already-locked success, real password unlock before a delayed first result read (one request, no re-lock), simultaneous callers sharing one pending token, missing PAM and unavailable shell preserving screensaver stand-ins, and restart rejecting old receipts.\n\nAt 3a590ca0, native activation verified 1,160 installed source files with zero mismatches and the running installed shell path. A secured token remained active for 31 seconds; a fresh already-locked request shared it, real PAM password unlock left its archived result secured, and a read 31 seconds later returned unknown without another request. The real system-lock command returned 0, the visible secured password surface was inspected, and the guest was left unlocked with its original idle preference restored.\n\nThe VM has no fingerprint hardware. Its fast-unlock check exercises the shared authenticated-unlock path with a delayed caller read. The accepted requested:true/never-secure timeout is covered by executable contract tests; the attempted native no-output setup was inconclusive because Hyprland retained a cached QScreen, and is not claimed as no-output evidence. Notification requests were checked; visible delivery is unavailable when the shell or outputs are absent.\n\nThis expands the script-only fix with request outcome reporting in the shell, addressing the ambiguity identified in the Oct 8 comparison with #13756. #13756 remains a separate maintainer choice; its accepted-but-never-secure limitation is not inferred to be fixed by this PR.\n","bodyDigest":"sha256:1937dffa19879ed473db717d942eb85cd756b63094da68f39ca3ca9f8f8f7a86","bodyExcerpt":"omarchy-system-lock now waits for a shell-issued receipt showing that its lock request became secure. The shell records that outcome before authentication can unlock again and retains it after unlock, so a fast authentication cannot cause the script to re-lock. Concurrent callers share the active request; tokens belong to one shell incarnation, and an unrelated or stale receipt cannot prove success. Existing lock lock IPC replies remain compatible.","source":"explicit","title":"Confirm each lock request became secure before reporting success"},"createdAt":"2026-10-10T03:37:41Z","decisionSummary":{"claimEvidence":[{"claim":"requirement_preserve